[Music]  this morning on the dish rising star  chef Whitney Ataka raised in a small  town in California's Mojave Desert she  earned a degree in anthropology before  deciding on a cooking career she's had  jobs at some of the nation's top  restaurants but fell in love with  Southern cuisine while working in Athens  Georgia now she makes her home not far  from there on Georgia's a rustic  Cumberland Island where she's executive  chef at the prestigious gray field in  and seaside cuisine is the subject of  her just-released first cookbook the  salt water table recipes from the  coastal south chef Whitney Otaka good  morning welcome to the dish every  morning great to be here this looks and  smells fantastic what do we have here  thank you I brought you a little taste  of the South East Coast starting on the  end there  we have buttermilk biscuits with a peach  and bay laurel JLo in the middle here we  have a very sort of traditional dish  it's fish shrimp and grits and we use  rice grits as a base as opposed to  traditional hominy grits next to it  there we have cast iron fillet beans  with a red pepper pepper rod and a been  a seed sprinkle on top right here we  have lovely fried oyster salad so trying  to make it a little more healthy with  some lettuces radishes milk dressing and  then right here we have a beautiful  grapefruit heart with chamomile cream  and often times we'll sprinkle a little  honey over that fantastic and then of  course the old man in the sea which is a  run bass dream grapefruit lime coconut  water and a little bit of salt mmm and  it's so good so I got to get to the  bottom of this you were studying at  Berkeley yeah and we you saw an ad in  the newspaper about a waitress job yeah  so I loved everything French growing up  as you know many many little girls do  and I was a Francophile and I saw an ad  for a waitressing position in Oakland  California little French Creperie which  I was like perfect I'm Amelie I'm going  to serving  beautiful food I went in and I applied  for the waitressing job  the owner is from Toulouse France you  can see clearly I had no waitressing  experience and he put me in the kitchen  with him which i thought was even more  brave than putting him before but he  really taught me so much about the  beauty of food the conversation of food  the shared table we would eat a meal  after every shift together and it was  just a lovely experience I fell in love  with restaurants and you can fall in  love where you work right now talk a  little bit about where that is yeah it's  Cumberland Island so it's basically the  size of Manhattan about 17 miles except  for there's 50 permanent residents  instead of however many I am live here  and it's a National Seashore designated  in 1972 I'm exposed to the nature of the  place every single day it inspires how I  choose to design the menus how I decide  to cook I'm outside I see things growing  wild things growing you know we have a  beautiful two acre garden that supplies  things so it very much influences how I  think about this so Whitney gets to work  on a national seashore and make amazing  food yeah yeah a little bit and gets to  work with her husband how does that go  because sometimes that can be a  challenge some people wouldn't say  that's such a lucky but for us it's  always worked we met working in  restaurants it's basically like having a  clone in the kitchen we think the same  we develop our menus the same way I  always have a sounding board to work off  of he's fabulous what's the biggest  challenge though to working here you  know I think that work never really ends  especially for someone like me that's  kind of a workaholic how we always carry  at home but we're always talking about  food so sometimes I step back and  realize how lucky that job is it's not a  terrible thing and what is it about  southern cuisine yeah it's you when I  moved to the South I was instantly  struck by the beauty of the cuisine the  history of the culture of the place  Agriculture's everywhere so I instantly  fell in love with it it's such a strong  food region so what is the dish that is  your favorite oh well that's just a  terrible question  [Laughter]  [Applause]  right now I I mean I was I always go  through waves with the biscuits well  lately I've been eating a million  biscuits  so maybe biscuits should be where  everyone should start with something I  enjoy as you sign the dish yeah if you  weren't gonna have this meal with anyone  past or present who would it be you know  a great influence on my writing and  someone I've always deeply admired was  Anthony Bourdain and when he passed it  was it struck me a lot and I realized  you know his voice always came to me and  I realized he really wrote in his off  time and made the time for it and it was  a drive for me so I would love to have  cooked for him in my life hear that name  yeah you signed that dish in chef  Whitney Ataka we thank you for being  here with us and if you want to know  more about Whitney or the dish as she  hands it to you go to the web site at  CBS this morning  dot-com

